Week 1 Task: Strategic Planning and Data Exploration in Logistics
Project Title
Logistics Performance Analysis and Delivery Delay Prediction

1. Introduction
Logistics and supply chain operations generate large amounts of data related to orders, customers, products, shipping, delivery performance, and geographical regions. Analyzing this information can help organizations identify operational bottlenecks, monitor delivery performance, reduce delays, and improve resource allocation.
This project focuses on analyzing logistics performance using publicly available supply chain data. The project follows a data-driven approach in which logistics KPIs are identified, the available data is explored, delivery-risk patterns are analyzed, and data science techniques are proposed for future predictive and optimization tasks.
The Week 1 task focuses primarily on strategic planning and initial data exploration. The analysis establishes the foundation for subsequent stages involving data cleaning, advanced exploratory analysis, predictive modeling, and optimization.

2. Logistics Scenario
Consider a global e-commerce and supply chain organization that handles thousands of orders across multiple geographical regions. The organization uses different shipping modes such as First Class, Same Day, Second Class, and Standard Class.
The company needs to monitor whether shipments are being delivered according to their expected schedules. Delivery delays can increase operational costs, affect customer satisfaction, and create additional pressure on logistics resources.
The proposed analytical project aims to answer questions such as:
•	Which shipping modes have higher delivery risk?
•	Which geographical regions experience higher delivery risk?
•	How large is the difference between actual and scheduled shipping time?
•	Which logistics KPIs should management monitor?
•	Can machine learning be used to predict delivery-delay risk?
•	How can data-driven insights support better logistics decisions?

3. Project Objectives
The main objectives of this project are:
1.	Analyze overall logistics and shipping performance.
2.	Identify important logistics KPIs.
3.	Compare actual and scheduled shipping times.
4.	Analyze delivery-risk patterns across shipping modes.
5.	Identify regions with relatively higher delivery risk.
6.	Explore relationships between logistics variables.
7.	Prepare the data for further analytical and predictive tasks.
8.	Explain how machine learning techniques can be applied to delivery-risk prediction.
9.	Explore how clustering and optimization could support logistics decision-making.
10.	Generate actionable business recommendations.

4. Background Research and Dataset
For this project, the DataCo SMART Supply Chain for Big Data Analysis dataset was selected as a publicly available supply-chain dataset.
The dataset contains transactional information related to orders, customers, products, sales, shipping, delivery performance, and geographical regions.
Important variables include:
•	Order ID
•	Customer ID
•	Order Region
•	Order Country
•	Shipping Mode
•	Actual Shipping Days
•	Scheduled Shipping Days
•	Delivery Status
•	Late Delivery Risk
•	Order Quantity
•	Product Price
•	Order Value
•	Order Profit
The dataset is appropriate for this project because it contains variables that can be used to investigate shipping performance, delivery risk, order characteristics, and regional logistics behavior.
Dataset Sources
The dataset was obtained from publicly available sources:
Mendeley Data: DataCo SMART SUPPLY CHAIN FOR BIG DATA ANALYSIS
Kaggle: DataCo SMART SUPPLY CHAIN FOR BIG DATA ANALYSIS
The raw dataset is used for analytical and educational purposes and is not included directly in the GitHub repository.

