6. Key Performance Indicators
The following KPIs were identified for monitoring logistics performance.
6.1 Total Orders
Measures the number of unique orders handled by the organization.
6.2 Total Order Records
Measures the total number of transactional records available for analysis.
6.3 Total Quantity Sold
Measures the total number of product units ordered.
6.4 Total Sales
Measures the total sales value generated by the orders.
6.5 Average Actual Shipping Time
Measures the average number of days required for actual shipment.
6.6 Average Scheduled Shipping Time
Measures the average number of days that shipments were expected to require.
6.7 Schedule Deviation
Measures the difference between actual and scheduled shipping time.
6.8 On-Time/Non-Late-Risk Rate
Measures the percentage of records where the Late_delivery_risk indicator is zero.
Important: This metric is calculated from the Late_delivery_risk indicator and should not be interpreted as a direct measurement of actual delivery status.

7. Data Exploration
The dataset was loaded into Python using Pandas.
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

file_path = "../data/DataCoSupplyChainDataset.csv"

df = pd.read_csv(file_path, encoding="latin1")

print("Dataset loaded successfully!")
print("Shape:", df.shape)
The initial analysis was performed to understand the size, structure, variables, and logistics-related characteristics of the dataset.

8. Initial KPI Results
The initial analysis produced the following results:
KPI	Result
Total Orders	65,752
Total Order Records	180,519
Total Quantity Sold	384,079
Total Sales	33,054,402.38
Average Actual Shipping Time	3.50 days
Average Scheduled Shipping Time	2.93 days
Average Schedule Deviation	0.57 days
On-Time/Non-Late-Risk Rate	45.17%
The results show that the average actual shipping time was higher than the average scheduled shipping time. This indicates a schedule deviation of approximately 0.57 days across the analyzed records.

9. Shipping Mode Analysis
Shipping modes were analyzed to identify differences in delivery-risk levels.
Shipping Mode	Shipments	Avg. Actual Days	Avg. Scheduled Days	Late-Risk Rate
First Class	27,814	2.00	1.00	95.32%
Same Day	9,737	0.48	0.00	45.74%
Second Class	35,216	3.99	2.00	76.63%
Standard Class	107,752	4.00	4.00	38.07%
Finding
First Class shipments showed the highest late-delivery-risk rate at 95.32%, followed by Second Class at 76.63%.
Standard Class had the lowest late-delivery-risk rate among the analyzed shipping modes at 38.07%.
This result indicates that shipping mode may be an important feature for future predictive modeling.
However, these results should not automatically be interpreted as proof that one shipping mode causes delays. Other factors such as region, order characteristics, and scheduling policies may also influence delivery risk.

10. Regional Analysis
Regional performance was analyzed using shipment count, average shipping time, late-delivery risk, and sales.
The top regions by late-delivery risk included:
Region	Shipments	Avg. Shipping Days	Late-Risk Rate
Central Africa	1,677	3.56	57.96%
South Asia	7,731	3.50	56.27%
East Africa	1,852	3.51	55.94%
Western Europe	27,109	3.50	55.85%
South of USA	4,045	3.49	55.77%
Eastern Europe	3,920	3.50	55.66%
East of USA	6,915	3.50	55.66%
Southeast Asia	9,539	3.50	55.53%
Central Asia	553	3.42	55.33%
West Asia	6,009	3.49	55.28%
Finding
Central Africa had the highest late-delivery-risk rate among the analyzed regions at 57.96%.
Several other regions also showed late-risk rates above 55%. These regions can be investigated further during the advanced analysis stage.

11. Visualizations
